  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para><emphasis>Three Different Forms of Linear Equations</emphasis></para>
<para>Work with a partner to answer 1 - 3.</para>

<!--Q#1-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the slope-intercept form of a linear equation? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(y = mx + b\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#2-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the point-slope form of a linear equation? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(y -y_1= m (x -x_1 )\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the standard form of a linear equation? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(Ax + By = C\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<para><emphasis>Using the Different Forms to Write Equivalent Equations</emphasis></para>
<para>Linear equations can be written in the different forms to create linear equations. Use the linear equation forms from 1 - 3 to help you complete the rest of the activity. Remember to show all of your work on your paper and to use inverse operations to move terms to different sides of the equation.</para>
<para>For 4 - 5, use the equation \(y-4=3(x+1)\).</para>

<!--Q#4-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write the equation in slope-intercept form. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(y=3x+7\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write the equation in standard form. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(3x-y = -7\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#6-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write \(4x -6y =12\) in slope-intercept form. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(y=\frac{4}{6}x-2\) orb \(y=\frac{2}{3}x-2\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
